00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.480
Uh, yes, this is stone.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.480
Hey stone.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.580
What's up?

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.730
Uh, Hey, David.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:26.140
Um, so to kind of set up my question, um,
nowadays we have just incredible technology.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:34.970
Um, you know, we have the technology to provide
clean energy, renewable energy, uh, you know,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:44.180
uh, create clean water, uh, vertical farms,
three D printed homes, uh, things of that

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.870
nature.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:56.129
And it seems like the problem is that programs
like this never pick up or uh, expand because

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:58.899
they are not profitable.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:02.949
How do we change people's mindset?

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:15.310
So they are willing to buy in towards programs
that would help them, but they only ever see

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.400
that initial cost.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.440
I don't know that I agree that they're not
profitable, they're just profitable for different

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.440
people.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.440
Let me give you some examples.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.110
Um, and, and a lot of this is speculative
and we're just not going to know for a few

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.110
years.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.140
So vertical farming, for example, huge promise.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.300
But what's the advantage to vertical farming?

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.330
Vertical farming can be done

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.550
enabled

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.640
vertical space often inside of a building,
including an urban centers.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.740
If you're a farmer in the Midwest, vertical
farming is not profitable for you because

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.880
you're in the Midwest with huge fields, not
in an urban area with a building where you

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:49.960
would actually do it.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.060
So vertical farming is profitable for different
people.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.000
The normal farming is profitable.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.970
For another example that you mentioned, three
D printing homes, three D printing homes,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.850
the technology, it's still early in that technology.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.220
I mean, you know, we've seen modular become
more of a thing.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.200
Uh, but three D printing is different.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.700
The people who would benefit from three D
printing homes are not the people who can

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.030
currently control the building industry and
big builders and contractors.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.680
So it's not that it's economically not viable
to three D print homes, although it's still

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.640
expensive today because we've not scaled yet.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.940
But the people who would benefit from it are
different than the ones who currently control

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.940
that industry.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.830
So I just think that what your ne, your assessment
is right, that there are misaligned incentives,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.380
but it's not that it's just not profitable
to go in these new directions is that it's

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.480
going be profitable for different people than
the ones that currently control those industries.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.480
Okay.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:57.460
Um, I guess kind of a short followup question,
um, I mean, if, if we, you know, we have the

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.670
power to end homelessness and end hunger

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.880
[inaudible]

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.520
we, we have the technology to do it, so, so
why doesn't it happen?

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.680
Lack of political will and that we've talked
about a lot.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.260
There's a lack of political will.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.120
Um, uh, always having another election around
the corner is a disincentive to deal with

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.730
some of these bigger picture issues that we
can solve except that there's not really the

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.400
political will to do it.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.500
Uh, that that's a systemic problem, which,
uh, which I've talked about many times before.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.030
Stone, thank you for the phone call.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.280
I really appreciate hearing [inaudible]

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.630
you.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:35.630
